preConditionHook:
// A DELIBERATE CONJUNCTION, carried against atomic-beats-composite because the
// USE IS THE NEGATION. Grimes's proof that a break was real is that the
// breakout bar BOTH expands its range beyond the recent mean AND trades
// above-average volume; his weak-breakout warning is literally
// NOT(range AND volume), and the negation of an AND is an OR, which two ANDed
// entry panes cannot express. That is the LargeOutsideBar /
// SmallWeakSignalBarBlock shape, and it is the whole reason this file exists:
// placed POSITIVELY it is reachable as two panes (BarRangeBelowAverageMultiple
// negated, plus the tick-count rule), placed NEGATED it is not
// reachable at all.
//
// Nothing in the catalog ANDs a range-expansion test with a volume test:
// ClimaxBar pairs range with a BODY, BreakoutBarLargeBodyLargeRange pairs it
// with the bar's own body fraction, and LargeRangeBarInWindow and
// VolumeAboveAverageMultiple are the two halves standing alone.
//
// THE TWO BENCHMARKS USE DIFFERENT WINDOWS, on purpose, because each half is
// spelled the way the corpus already spells it:
//   * RANGE -- the mean bar range over the window ENDING ONE BAR AGO, so an
//     oversized breakout bar cannot raise its own benchmark; the EL twin's
//     indexed-Average idiom at offset 1, which
//     BarRangeBelowAverageMultiple and BreakoutBarLargeBodyLargeRange write
//     exactly this way. The bar range is a PRICE expression with real bar
//     history, so this is a FRESH loop over price -- not a rolling accumulator
//     and not a mirrored variable buffer. Summed newest-first, EL's Summation
//     order.
//   * TICK COUNT -- vol = ctx.Ticks (up-ticks + down-ticks; NOT the `volume`
//     alias, which mirrors EL's up-ticks-only word -- see the `ticks` row in
//     EL_FEATURES.md), and its average over the CURRENT-INCLUSIVE window,
//     which is VolumeAboveAverageMultiple's measured spelling. vol is an EL
//     VARIABLE, so that average reads its zero-filled warm-up history --
//     mirrored here as a zero-initialized ring buffer, exactly as
//     BarRangeAboveStd does, re-seeded when the depth changes.
// Mixing the two windows is stated rather than assumed: shifting the volume
// average would mean lagging an Average taken over a VARIABLE, a compound
// behaviour the register does not cover.
//
// SIDES IDENTICAL: a range and a tick count carry no direction.
//
// Ranges and counts only, so the rule passes the shift test.
//
// Every scratch value is a localVariables entry or loop-scoped, never a
// hook-scope declaration, so the hooks survive a double placement.
//
// Deepest bar touched: lookback.
avgRange = 0.0;
for (int i = 0; i < (int)lookback; ++i) {
    avgRange += high[1 + i] - low[1 + i];
}
avgRange /= (int)lookback;
vol = (double)ctx.Ticks[0];
if (volHistory.size() != (std::size_t)(int)lookback) {
    volHistory.assign((std::size_t)(int)lookback, 0.0);
    volCursor = 0;
}
volHistory[volCursor] = vol;
volCursor = (volCursor + 1) % volHistory.size();
avgVol = 0.0;
for (double v : volHistory) {
    avgVol += v;
}
avgVol /= (int)lookback;
